{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1776883507,"nonce":"3e+eU7F7V30/MNJcCYi/lRtm6HE=","title":"Bug: session status not reset when container starts successfully","message":"## Problem\n\nWhen a session has `suspended:provider-overload` status from previous failed attempts, and a new container is launched that successfully connects to the provider, the status is not reset to `working`.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n1. Session 9c87883d84c6 had `overload_resumes: 5` from earlier OpenRouter failures\n2. Config was fixed to use OpenAI directly\n3. `nightshift resume` launched a new container\n4. Container successfully connected and processed 127+ items\n5. Status remained `suspended:provider-overload` even though container was actively working\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nWhen `nightshift resume` launches a container that successfully starts streaming events, the status should be reset to `working` and `overload_resumes` should be cleared.\n\n## Actual Behavior\n\nStatus stays at `suspended:provider-overload` from previous runs. The watcher sees this stale status and may interfere with a working session.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nThe status is set in state.json by the adapter when it detects overload, but there's no code path that resets it when:\n1. A new container is launched via `nightshift resume`\n2. The agent successfully starts streaming events\n\n## Files\n\n- `host/launch.py` or `host/workspace_setup.py` - should reset status on resume\n- `core/session.py` - could reset status when first successful event arrives\n- `core/state.py` - manages state persistence\n\nREQ: REQ-033 (provider overload handling)","files":null}]}